  "worked_solution": "Let ${X}_{i}$ be the $i$th score obtained from the first spinner, and let ${Y}_{j}$ be the $j$th score obtained from the second spinner. Then, we need to calculate \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{X}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{5}{Y}_{j}].\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expected value, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{X}_{i}+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{5}{Y}_{j}] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}\\text{E}[{X}_{i}]+{\\sum }_{j=1}^{5}\\text{E}[{Y}_{j}] \\\\  & =10\\text{E}[X]+5\\text{E}[Y],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the last equality above is true because $\\text{E}[{X}_{i}]=\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}_{j}]=\\text{E}[Y]$ for all $1\\le i\\le 10,\\,1\\le j\\le 5.$\n\nThe expected values of $X$ and $Y$ are calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.2=1.8 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & =0\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.3+4\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.1=1.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[10X+5Y]=10\\text{E}[X]+5\\text{E}[Y]=10\\cdot 1.8+5\\cdot 1.8=27.\n$$",
